==Russian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ru|имѣ́ть||ru-PRO}}

===Etymology===
{{root|ru|ine-pro|*h₁em-}}
{{inh+|ru|sla-pro|*jьměti}}. Compare {{cog|be|мець}}, {{cog|bg|имам}}, {{cog|uk|ма́ти}}, {{cog|pl|mieć}}, {{cog|sh|имати}}/{{m|sh|imati}}, {{cog|cu|имѣти}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA|име́ть}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-иметь.ogg}}

===Verb===
{{ru-verb|име́ть|impf|vn=име́ние}}

# to [[have]], to [[possess]]
#: {{uxi|ru|[[я|Я]] [[хотеть|хочу́]] [[име́ть]] [[друг|друзе́й]].|I want '''to have''' friends.}}
#* {{RQ:RSB|Mark|6|18|Ибо Иоанн говорил Ироду: не должно тебе '''иметь''' жену брата твоего.|t=John had said to Herod, “It is not lawful for you '''to have''' your brother’s wife.”}}
# {{lb|ru|euphemism|colloquial}} to [[fuck]], to [[have]]
#: {{uxi|ru|[[име́ть]] [[кто-то|кого́-то]]|'''to fuck''' someone|lit='''to have''' someone}}
# {{lb|ru|archaic}} to [[have to]]; to be [[obliged]] (to do something)

====Usage notes====
Иметь is rarely used in Russian with the sense of 'have', except with abstract concepts, most often in set expressions ({{m|ru|име́ть в виду́|4=to mean, to have in mind}}, {{m|ru|[[име́ть]] [[права́]]||to have rights}}, etc.). The most common way to express possession is "{{m|ru|у}} + possessor [in the genitive] + {{m|ru|есть}} + possession [in the nominative]". However, {{m|ru||иметь}} is commonly used when an infinitive is needed, as in the above example sentence.

